S7, S4: mdns-sd as Sukkula patches it; sources filtered, and counted

Two changes in how this crate uses mdns-sd, which Sukkula now vendors
with patches of its own (third_party/mdns-sd.patches: a bounded cache,
no timer per record, capped TTLs, only the local link read, unicast
replies limited, the announcing source reported):

- Both daemons get the embedding application's reach policy as a source
  filter (`ServiceDaemon::new_with_source_filter`): a packet from an
  address the policy refuses is dropped unread, so the responder never
  answers one -- a legacy-unicast query from outside the LAN used to get
  a reply sent straight back to it -- and discovery never caches one.
- The discovery cache was first come, first served: once 64 services
  were remembered, nobody new was, for as long as the first comers kept
  announcing -- one host with 64 instance names was enough. Now one
  source (the packets' source address, `EndpointInfo::source`, not the
  address an announcement names) keeps at most 4, a new one replacing
  its own oldest, and a newcomer at a full cache takes the place of the
  oldest service of the source holding the most, if that source keeps
  more than the newcomer then has. Services replaced are reported gone.

tests/mdns.rs: a responder whose policy admits one address answers a
legacy-unicast query from it, and not from another loopback address,
and a burst of twelve gets at most four replies; a host announcing 1500
devices at TTL 2^32-1 plus records for names nobody browses leaves at
most four of its services listed and a handful of records and timers in
the daemon, and a phone announcing itself afterwards is listed, with
its own source.

Findings: Sukkula review 2026-09-25 kept[15], kept[1], kept[6].
